## `jq` skill

`jq` is installed. Use it to process JSON from `goof` and
other CLI tools; `goof` emits JSON by default.

Filters compose with `|`. `.` is the input; use `.field`,
`.[n]`, `.[:n]`, and `.[]` to access, index, slice, and
iterate. Add `?` to optional traversal (`.items[]?`). Use
`{id, title}` to project fields, `{id: .id[:8], model}` to
rename, and `[filter]` to collect results into an array.

Use `select(predicate)` with comparisons and functions such
as `startswith("prefix")`, `test("regex"; "i")`, and
`any(predicate)`. Guard nullable arrays before iterating:

```bash
cmd | jq '.[] | select(.tags != null) | select(.tags | any(startswith("net/"))) | {id, title}'
```

Use `// empty` to omit missing or null values:

```bash
cmd | jq -r '.osv.affected[]?.ecosystem_specific.imports[]?.symbols[]? // empty'
```

Use `length` to count, `-r` for raw strings, `-c` for one
compact value per line, and `-s` to collect NDJSON inputs
into an array.
